## Goal
Create a principal component analysis (PCA) plot of the six RNA-seq samples from the gene-level counts, with points coloured by condition (untreated or treated), to show the major patterns of sample-to-sample variation.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper (native) | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |

## Method
1. Run a Python script under the `gars-bio` environment on the batch executor. Read the resolved gene count TSV, use its sample columns and retain only gene identifier and count columns; exclude non-count annotation columns (such as gene name). Match sample columns to the project design in `00_data/rnaseq_bulk/samples.csv` and use the `condition` values as plot labels. Stop with an informative error if the sample columns do not match the six design sample IDs or if counts are not numeric.
2. For each gene, calculate counts per million (CPM) across samples using each sample's library total. Retain genes with CPM >= 1 in at least three samples. Transform retained values as log2(CPM + 1), center and scale each gene across samples, then calculate the first two principal components with scikit-learn `PCA(n_components=2)` on the samples-by-genes matrix. Do not use condition labels in feature selection or PCA fitting.
3. Save a scatter plot of PC1 versus PC2 with each sample labelled by sample ID and coloured by condition, including the variance explained in each axis label and a legend. Also save a table of sample ID, condition, PC1 and PC2. Do not interpret or perform inferential tests on the separation.

## Outputs
Types come from the closed vocabulary in _references/artifact_types.md.

